{
  "requirement": "For a requirement with `{sites}` or `{violations}` queries, the review prompt MUST direct the reviewer to apply the guidance of {cite}`requirements.cli.distributed_guidance`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject has one obligation. For a requirement with `{sites}` or `{violations}` queries, the review prompt MUST direct the reviewer to apply the guidance of `requirements.cli.distributed_guidance`. The guidance itself is the bundled skills' explanation of distributed requirements, which is covered by its own requirement.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_review_prompt_applies_the_distributed_guidance]` is marked `{test}` for the subject. It reads the review prompt through `review_prompt`, which loads the habeas-requirements skill's `references/review-prompt.md`. It then extracts the `## Criteria` section with `section`. That helper collapses the section body and stops at the next heading of the same or higher level, skipping code fences. `assert_text` then requires that body to contain this exact phrase: \"for a requirement with `{sites}` or `{violations}` queries, apply the Do and Don't lists in the habeas skill's `references/distributed-requirements.md`\".\n\nThe assertion covers each part of the obligation:\n- the condition, which is a requirement with `{sites}` or `{violations}` queries;\n- the directive to the reviewer, which is \"apply\";\n- the target guidance, which is the Do and Don't lists in the distributed-requirements reference.\n\nThe assertion is scoped to the Criteria section, where the reviewer's instructions live. The test fails with a clear message if the section or phrase is missing. A prompt that omitted the directive, dropped the condition, or pointed at the wrong reference would fail it.\n\nThe requirement has no other modes or boundary cases. The test is a single, sufficient presence check on the prompt text. I found no gaps and no marking problems.",
